<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Hatay State</h1><p class="st-meta">1938 CE – 1939 CE · capital Antakya</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A transitional state created from the Sanjak of Alexandretta in 1938, which joined Turkey within a year.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After elections held under Turkish military supervision gave Turks a majority, the assembly of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sanjak-of-alexandretta">Sanjak of Alexandretta</a> proclaimed the State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatay-province">Hatay</a> in September 1938, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antakya</a> (<a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-antioch">Antioch</a>) as capital. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, seeking Turkish support against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, accepted the change.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tayfur-sokmen">Tayfur Sokmen</a> was elected head of state and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdurrahman-melek">Abdurrahman Melek</a> prime minister. The state used a flag modelled on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey's</a>, adopted Turkish laws and currency and was closely tied to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a>, functioning as a stage towards annexation rather than as an independent country.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>On 29 June 1939 the assembly voted to join <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, which formally annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatay-province">Hatay</a> in July. Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> left for <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lebanese-republic">Lebanon</a>, including the Armenians of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musa-dagh">Musa Dagh</a>, who resettled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anjar">Anjar</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/hatay-state.jpg" alt="Hatay State" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: A. Özgür Erdemli · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Hatay.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1938 CE – 1939 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Antakya</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tayfur Sökmen, Abdurrahman Melek</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam, Alawite Islam, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Turkish, Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Hatay, State of Hatay</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1938&amp;focus=hatay-state" aria-label="Hatay State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5591 871 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Hatay State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5911 1127L5912 1120M5911 1127L5809 1005"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5912 1120h0M5809 1005h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5911 1127h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5911 1127h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Antakya. <a href="/atlas?year=1938&amp;focus=hatay-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
